        "content": {
          "content_type": "text",
          "parts": [
            "The **generic name for Zofran** is **\ue200entity\ue202[\"medication\",\"ondansetron\",\"anti-nausea medication\"]\ue201**.\n\n### What it is used for:\n- Prevents **nausea and vomiting**\n- Commonly prescribed after:\n  - Surgery  \n  - Chemotherapy  \n  - Radiation  \n  - Severe migraines or illness-related nausea  \n\n### How it works:\nOndansetron blocks **serotonin (5-HT3) receptors**, which are responsible for triggering nausea and vomiting signals in the body.\n\n### Common forms:\n- Tablets  \n- Orally dissolving tablets (ODT)  \n- Liquid  \n- IV (in hospital settings)\n\n### Quick note for you (given your recent surgery):\nOndansetron is often part of post-op care, especially if you're taking pain meds like opioids, which can cause nausea.\n\nIf you're asking because you're deciding whether to take it again right now, tell me:\n- what time you last took it  \n- your current symptoms  \n\nI can help you time it safely with your other meds."
          ]
